# Production synchronized transfer/intake feeding

MainTeleOp and all 12 shooting autos now use `ShootingCoordinator` and `SynchronizedFeed`.
The existing distance-to-flywheel-speed function is preserved. Launch boost and adaptive
compensation are not part of production feeding. Transfer now uses the validated voltage
model (kS=1.1294224, kV=.004082160, kP=.001) with a final target of 1440 ticks/s.

## Sequence

1. Collect and store the usual 3-4 balls in the tunnel, with transfer stopped.
2. Flywheel remains within +/-25 ticks/s of its current target for 200 ms.
3. Intake and transfer start together. Transfer ramps from 0 to 1440 ticks/s over
   300 ms; intake runs at its battery-compensated calibrated 85% setting from the
   first positive transfer-ramp command.
4. Both feed motors continue for the entire stored batch, including ordinary shot dips.
   There is no transfer-only pre-spin, transfer-speed gate, two-second cutoff, or
   artificial recovery pause between balls in a batch.
5. Trigger release or the autonomous timed command ends the batch and stops both feed motors.
6. A new batch must pass the flywheel readiness dwell again before either feed motor starts.

Without a ball sensor, software cannot verify that the tunnel is empty or count the
balls fired. TeleOp preserves hold-trigger batch feeding; the operator releases after
the tunnel empties. Autos use explicit durations, which must be checked with real 3-4
ball batches. These durations are not a guarantee of emptying. The previous single-ball
tests cannot establish timing or recovery for a whole stored batch.

Releasing shoot, turning off flywheel, or STOP immediately cancels feeding. Once a batch
is feeding, distance-target changes no longer automatically interrupt it. The next batch
still uses the normal readiness dwell. Readiness waits have no automatic timeout.

Automatic production shooting fault lockouts have been removed at the user's request.
Encoder/battery validation, motor-mode mismatch, reverse-direction checks, stall detection,
loop timing and readiness/recovery timeout checks no longer latch faults or shut down
operator commands. Stalls can continue receiving motor power until the operator stops.

Missing/nonpositive battery data uses the last valid positive voltage (initially 12.76 V).
Invalid velocity samples retain the last finite measured velocity (initially zero).
These substituted readings are not proof of valid sensor data. Motor commands remain
finite and bounded; the flywheel PI trim and normal ready-speed synchronization remain.
Explicit operator/test aborts and file-integrity aborts in diagnostic OpModes still work;
these are separate from automatic production sensor/stall lockouts. Other standalone
tuning OpModes retain their own experimental validation logic.

The coordinator owns intake and transfer requests. Launcher no longer configures transfer
hardware.

The coordinator also owns Launcher.update(): forward and reverse motor updates happen
in its corresponding branch. B flywheel reversal is set and applied directly inside
`if (outtake || eject)` via `launcher.outtake()`. The flag setter is private;
`launcher.stopOuttake()` handles release without disabling an already-forward launcher.
MainTeleOp passes the Y-toggle edge to the coordinator and
only prepares the pose-based target; AutoCommands also prepares the target without
separately writing flywheel power. This prevents a forward update immediately before
an outtake update. Legacy standalone tuners may continue calling Launcher.update().

## MainTeleOp controls

- G2 Y: flywheel toggle.
- Hold G2 right trigger (>0.2): synchronized shooting at the full tuned transfer target.
  Trigger depth no longer scales shooting transfer velocity.
- G2 left trigger: manual intake collection when shoot is not requested.
- G2 B: reverse intake, transfer and flywheel (transfer/flywheel at -40%), overriding shooting.
  Forward flywheel requests and PI trim are cleared. Release both B and right bumper to stop reversal;
  press Y to enable forward flywheel control again. Explicit operator stops still apply.
- G2 right bumper: reverse all three motors, exactly like B. Either button, or both held,
  overrides shooting, collection and a simultaneous Y-toggle request.
- G2 BACK or G1 B: stop launcher/transfer/intake.
- Fresh G2 BACK+X: stopped shooting-fault reset. Re-enable flywheel explicitly afterward.
- G2 A ramp toggle and G2 X hold-balls servo control remain as before.

Telemetry shows synchronized-feed state, actual feeding and the shared fault message.

## Autonomous

`AutoCommands.shootForMs(...)` waits for the requested **continuous batch feeding duration**.
The simultaneous intake/transfer ramp counts as feeding; preceding flywheel warmup does
not. Interrupted commands cancel feeding immediately. The automatic wall-time watchdog
has been removed. A readiness wait can therefore continue until speed is ready or the
operator stops the OpMode.
The original requested 1500/3000/5000 ms feeding budgets are retained, but **total auto
elapsed time can increase** due to the flywheel readiness check. Recheck autonomous timing
on the robot before competition; path geometry is unchanged. A shooting fault ends the
shoot command and disables all shooting motors; it does not cancel subsequent drive paths.

Collection commands issue collection requests to the coordinator, so they cannot bypass
the intake gate during a shot. All 12 shooting autos share the same Launcher instance
owned by AutoCommands for both control and telemetry. Motion-only diagnostic autos are
unchanged. Existing tuning OpModes continue to test their explicitly selected experiments.

## First robot check / stored-batch CSV validation

Use **Shooting | Stored Batch Validation** in Diagnostics after building/installing.
Keep aim/distance fixed and test the usual stored batches of 3-4 balls.
The intake ramp servo starts UP, matching the earlier shooting validation geometry.

1. INIT then START. Motor commands remain zero until loading is confirmed.
2. At LOAD, hold G2 left trigger to collect into the tunnel. Release it when loaded.
   Use G2 LB/RB to set the actual loaded count (1-4; normally 3 or 4).
3. Press G2 A once. Observe WAIT_FLYWHEEL -> RAMP_TRANSFER -> FEED. Both intake
   and transfer must start together after readiness and stay running for the whole batch.
4. Press G2 A again once the tunnel is visibly empty. Both feed motors stop immediately;
   the test waits for flywheel recovery, then switches off the flywheel and shows RESULT.
   There is no assumed automatic empty time. G1 B aborts at any time.
5. At RESULT use G2 UP/DOWN to select how many balls hit (starts at zero), then G2 X
   to confirm. G2 LEFT repeats an invalid batch. Mark an incomplete/uncertain batch invalid.
6. Complete 3 valid batches, then STOP. G1 X resets a resolved fault to LOAD.
   Storage faults require restarting the OpMode.

Send all three `stored-batch-feed-TIMESTAMP*` files from
`Android/data/org.firstinspires.ftc.teamcode/files/flywheel-tuning/`.
Sample logs contain production state, loaded ball count, target/measured speeds, motor
powers, intake state, battery and accumulated feed time. Trial logs contain batch hit
counts, motor metrics and feed duration ending at the operator's empty confirmation.
That duration includes human reaction delay and is not an exact ball exit timestamp.
The report includes each scored batch. Loaded and hit counts are operator-reported.

## Desktop verification

- `python3 tests/shooting/run.py`: deterministic sequencing plus production controller tests
  using real SDK interfaces and fake motors. Checks ramp, speed dwells, dips, release,
  readiness, target changes, manual stops, operator priorities, and that missing sensor
  data/zero intake velocity no longer cause automatic fault lockouts.
- `python3 tests/shooting/check_compile.py`: compiles every TeamCode Java source against
  cached Android/FTC/Pedro dependencies in a temporary directory without Gradle writes.
- `python3 tests/flywheel/run.py`: existing flywheel/tuning/diagnostic regression suite.

Desktop tests do not validate physical feeding or autonomous field timing.
